<section xml:id="sec-ch04-02-ScalarLineIntegrals" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Scalar Line Integrals</title>

  <note>
    <p>
      As we saw in Calculus I, given an integrable function
      <m>p(x), \int_{a}^{b} p(x) d x</m> calculates the net area bounded by
      <m>p(x)</m> and the <m>x</m>-axis on <m>[a, b]</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      In this section, we will find a way to find the net area bounded by a
      surface <m>f(x, y)</m> and the <m>x y</m>-plane, restricted to a curve
      <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> in its domain.
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-02-ex-15-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Below you can see the net area (in light green) bounded by the
        surface <m>f(x, y)=y^2\cos x</m> (in purple), restricted to the
        curve <m>y=\sqrt{1-x^{2}}-2</m> (in blue).
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-02-ex-15-1">
        <caption>A curve C in xyz-space with a scalar function f(x,y,z) evaluated along it, illustrating the scalar line integral.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_09_748feeae06a4ec211266g-1. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-02-ex-15-1" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_09_748feeae06a4ec211266g-1.png">
              <shortdescription>A curve C in xyz-space with a scalar function f(x,y,z) evaluated along it, illustrating the scalar line integral.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>A curve <m>C</m> in <m>xyz</m>-space with a scalar function <m>f(x,y,z)</m> evaluated along it, illustrating the scalar line integral.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      You can interpret this as finding the net area of a wall with height
      given by <m>f(x, y)</m>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Arc Length as a Function</title>

    <p>
      Suppose you have an integrable curve
      <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle f(t), g(t)\rangle</m> on <m>[a, b]</m> (that is
      traversed only once). For any <m>a \lt t \leq b</m>, we can calculate
      the arc length of the curve on <m>[a, t]</m> using the formula
      <men xml:id="men-ch04-02-arc-length-function">
        s(t)=\int_{a}^{t}\left|\vec{r}^{\prime}(\tau)\right| d \tau
      </men>
    </p>

    <p>
      Moreover, by the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, we have that
      <men xml:id="men-ch04-02-arc-length-derivative">
        s^{\prime}(t)=\frac{d s}{d t}=\frac{d}{d t}\left(\int_{a}^{t}\left|\vec{r}^{\prime}(\tau)\right| d \tau\right)=\left|r^{\prime}(t)\right|
      </men>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Equivalently,
      <men xml:id="men-ch04-02-arc-length-differential">
        d s=\left|\vec{r}^{\prime}(t)\right| d t
      </men>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <p>
    Equation <xref ref="men-ch04-02-arc-length-differential"/> is what we
    call the arc length differential.
  </p>

  <p>
    Now, we are ready to find the net area bounded by <m>f(x, y)</m> and
    the <m>x y</m>-plane restricted to the curve <m>\vec{r}(t)</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    We will do the same thing we did in Calculus I: we will find the net
    area by adding net areas of rectangles and taking a limit at the end.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-02-rectangles">
    <caption>The more rectangles we use, the better the approximation</caption>

    <sidebyside widths="30% 30% 30%" margins="auto" valign="middle">
      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-02-rectangles-a">
        <caption>The net area bounded by f on R, approximated using 8 rectangles.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to Area under a curve _ Desmos_000366. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-02-rectangles-a" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <!--<instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>-->
          <static>
            <image source="/images/Area-under-a-curve-_-Desmos_000366.png">
              <shortdescription>The net area bounded by f on R, approximated using 8 rectangles.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The net area bounded by <m>f</m> on <m>R</m>, approximated using 8 rectangles.</p>
              </description>
