            <image source="/images/Area-under-a-curve-_-Desmos_000368.png">
              <shortdescription>The net area bounded by f on R, approximated using 32 rectangles.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The net area bounded by <m>f</m> on <m>R</m>, approximated using 32 rectangles.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </sidebyside>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The graphs above show only the plots; you can drag and zoom them. To see the expressions behind them, or to save a copy and create your own version of the graphs, open them in a new page from here:
    <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the left graph</url>,
    <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the middle graph</url>,
    <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the right graph</url>
  </p>

  <p>
    The first thing we do is partition the curve <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> into
    <m>n</m> pieces. Each piece will correspond to the base of a rectangle.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-02-partitioned-curve">
    <caption>Openstax Calculus 3, Section 6.2</caption>

    <image source="/images/2025_10_09_748feeae06a4ec211266g-3.jpg" width="50%">
      <shortdescription>A curve segment partitioned into smaller pieces.</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>A curve segment partitioned into smaller pieces.</p>
      </description>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    The curve <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> (in blue) partitioned into <m>n</m> pieces
    of length <m>\Delta s_{i}</m> (from OpenStax Vol 3)
  </p>

  <p>
    Each rectangle has base length <m>\Delta s_{k}</m> and height
    <m>f\left(x\left(t_{k}\right), y\left(t_{k}\right)\right)</m>, where
    <m>\left(x\left(t_{k}\right), y\left(t_{k}\right)\right)</m> is a point
    on the <m>k</m>-th piece of the <m>\vec{r}(t)</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Hence, an approximation for the net area is given by
    <md>
      \sum_{k=1}^{n} f\left(x\left(t_{k}\right), y\left(t_{k}\right)\right) \Delta s_{k}
    </md>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Finally, as we saw in the picture above (and know from Calculus I), if
    we let <m>n</m> approach infinity (the number of rectangles), the
    approximation will improve. Hence, if
    <md>
      \lim _{n \rightarrow \infty} \sum_{k=1}^{n} f\left(x\left(t_{k}\right), y\left(t_{k}\right)\right) \Delta s_{k}
    </md>
    exists, this is how we define the net area and we call this infinite
    sum a line integral.
  </p>

  <definition>
    <title>Scalar Line Integral</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose you have a surface <m>f(x, y)</m> defined on a smooth curve
        <m>C</m> given by
        <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle x(t), y(t)\rangle, a \leq t \leq b</m>. The
        line integral of <m>f</m> over <m>C</m> is
        <md>
          \lim _{n \rightarrow \infty} \sum_{k=1}^{n} f\left(x\left(t_{k}\right), y\left(t_{k}\right)\right) \Delta s_{k}=\int_{C} f(x(t), y(t)) d s
        </md>
        provided that the limit exists. If the limit exists, we say that
        <m>f</m> is integrable on <m>C</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <note>
    <p>
      As in previous examples, <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> being smooth means that it
      is differentiable (has no sharp edges and doesn't cross/intersect
      itself).
    </p>

    <p>
      From our work in the first remark, we know that
      <m>d s=\left|\vec{r}^{\prime}(t)\right| d t</m>, hence, we have that
      <md>
        \int_{C} f(x(t), y(t)) d s=\int_{a}^{b} f(x(t), y(t))\left|\vec{r}^{\prime}(t)\right| d t
      </md>
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Evaluating Line Integrals</title>

    <p>
      Let <m>f(x, y)</m> be continuous on a region containing a smooth curve
      <m>C</m> given by
      <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle x(t), y(t)\rangle, a \leq t \leq b</m>. Then
      <md>
        <mrow>\int_{C} f d s \amp =\int_{a}^{b} f(x(t), y(t))\left|\vec{r}^{\prime}(t)\right| d t</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp =\int_{a}^{b} f(x(t), y(t)) \sqrt{x^{\prime}(t)^{2}+y^{\prime}(t)^{2}} d t</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-02-ex-15-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate the line integral <m>\int_{C} x y d s</m>, where <m>C</m>
        is the quarter circle
        <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle \cos (t), \sin (t)\rangle, 0 \leq t \leq \frac{\pi}{2}</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-02-ex-15-2">
        <caption>The net area bounded by the surface z equals x times y, over the domain defined by the curve C.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_09_748feeae06a4ec211266g-5. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-02-ex-15-2" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_09_748feeae06a4ec211266g-5.png">
              <shortdescription>The net area bounded by the surface z equals x times y, over the domain defined by the curve C.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The net area bounded by the surface <m>z=xy</m>, over the domain defined by the curve <m>C</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Net area bounded by <m>f(x, y)</m> and the <m>x y</m>-plane,
        restricted to <m>\vec{r}(t)</m>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        For <m>\vec{r}(t) = \langle \cos t, \sin t\rangle</m> we have
        <m>x(t)=\cos t</m> and <m>y(t)=\sin t</m>, so
        <m>\vec{r}'(t) = \langle -\sin t, \cos t\rangle</m> and
        <md>
          |\vec{r}'(t)| = \sqrt{(-\sin t)^2 + (\cos t)^2} = \sqrt{\sin^2 t + \cos^2 t} = 1
        </md>,
        so <m>ds = |\vec{r}'(t)|\, dt = dt</m>. Substituting <m>x(t)</m>,
        <m>y(t)</m>, and <m>ds</m> into the line integral gives
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\int_{C} xy\, ds = \int_0^{\pi/2} \cos t \sin t\, dt =</mrow>
          <mrow>u=\sin t,\quad du = \cos t\, dt, \quad t=0 \Rightarrow u=0, \quad t=\frac{\pi}{2} \Rightarrow u=1</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_0^1 u\, du = \left. \frac{u^2}{2}\right|_0^1 = \frac{1}{2}</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      To parametrize a line segment starting at the point <m>P</m> and
      ending at the point <m>Q</m>, you can use the parametrization
      <md>
        P(1-t)+Q t, \quad 0 \leq t \leq 1
      </md>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-02-ex-15-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate the line integral
        <m>\int_{C}\left(x^{2}-y^{2}\right) d s</m>, where <m>C</m> is the
        line segment from <m>(2,0)</m> to <m>(0,1)</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-02-ex-15-3">
        <caption>The net area bounded by the surface z equals x squared minus y squared, over the domain defined by the curve C.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_09_748feeae06a4ec211266g-7. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-02-ex-15-3" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_09_748feeae06a4ec211266g-7.png">
              <shortdescription>The net area bounded by the surface z equals x squared minus y squared, over the domain defined by the curve C.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The net area bounded by the surface <m>z=x^2-y^2</m>, over the domain defined by the curve <m>C</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Net area bounded by <m>f(x, y)</m> and the <m>x y</m>-plane,
        restricted to <m>\vec{r}(t)</m>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The line segment from <m>P=(2,0)</m> to <m>Q=(0,1)</m> can be
        parametrized as
        <md>
          \vec{r}(t) = \langle 2,0\rangle + t\langle -2,1\rangle = \langle 2-2t,\, t\rangle, \ 0\leq t \leq 1, \ \vec{r}'(t) = \langle -2,1\rangle
        </md>,
        so <m>x(t)=2-2t</m> and <m>y(t)=t</m>. Then
        <md>
          |\vec{r}'(t)| = \sqrt{(-2)^2+1^2} = \sqrt{5}
        </md>,
        so <m>ds = \sqrt{5}\, dt</m>. Since
        <md>
          x(t)^2 - y(t)^2 = (2-2t)^2 - t^2 = 4-8t+4t^2-t^2 = 4-8t+3t^2
        </md>,
        the line integral becomes
        <md>
          \int_{C}(x^2-y^2)\, ds = \sqrt{5}\int_0^1 (4-8t+3t^2)\, dt = \sqrt{5}\left. \left(4t-4t^2+t^3\right)\right|_0^1 = \sqrt{5}(4-4+1) = \sqrt{5}
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      In <xref ref="example-ch04-02-ex-15-2"/> we see that part of the area
      is below the <m>x y</m>-plane. This area is still being added, but as
      a negative number.
    </p>

    <p>
      Hence, if <m>\int_{C} f d s \gt 0</m>, it doesn't mean that all of the
      area is above the <m>x y</m>-plane when restricted to <m>C</m>, it
      just means that most of the area is above the <m>x y</m>-plane.
    </p>
  </note>

  <p>
    When computing line integrals, it doesn't matter how you parametrize
    the curve <m>C</m>.
  </p>

  <remark>
    <title>Independence of Parametrization</title>

    <p>
      Let <m>f(x, y)</m> be continuous on a region containing a smooth curve
      <m>C</m>. Then the value of <m>\int_{C} f d s</m> is the same, no
      matter what parametrization you use for <m>C</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-02-ex-15-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Verify that <m>\int_{C}\left(x^{2}-y^{2}\right) d s</m>, where
        <m>C</m> is the line segment from <m>(2,0)</m> to <m>(0,1)</m>, has
        the same value as in <xref ref="example-ch04-02-ex-15-3"/> if you
        parametrize <m>C</m> as
        <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle -4t^2+2, 2t^2\rangle,0 \leq t \leq \frac{\sqrt 2}{2}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        For <m>\vec{r}(t) = \langle -4t^2+2, 2t^2\rangle</m> we have
        <m>x(t)=2-4t^2</m> and <m>y(t)=2t^2</m>; at <m>t=0</m> this gives
        <m>(2,0)</m> and at <m>t=\frac{\sqrt2}{2}</m> it gives <m>(0,1)</m>,
        so <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> traces the same line segment <m>C</m> as in
        <xref ref="example-ch04-02-ex-15-3"/>. Differentiating,
        <m>\vec{r}'(t) = \langle -8t, 4t\rangle</m>, so
        <md>
          |\vec{r}'(t)| = \sqrt{(-8t)^2+(4t)^2} = \sqrt{80t^2} = 4\sqrt{5}\,|t| = 4\sqrt{5}\,t
        </md>,
        since <m>t\geq 0</m> on <m>\left[0,\frac{\sqrt2}{2}\right]</m>. Hence
        <m>ds = 4\sqrt{5}\,t\, dt</m>. Since
        <md>
          x(t)^2-y(t)^2 = (2-4t^2)^2 - (2t^2)^2 = 4-16t^2+16t^4-4t^4 = 4-16t^2+12t^4
        </md>,
        the line integral becomes
        <md>
          \int_{C}(x^2-y^2)\, ds = \int_0^{\sqrt2/2} (4-16t^2+12t^4)(4\sqrt{5}\,t)\, dt = 4\sqrt{5}\int_0^{\sqrt2/2} \left(4t-16t^3+12t^5\right) dt
        </md>.
        Evaluating the antiderivative:
        <md>
          4\sqrt{5}\left. \left(2t^2-4t^4+2t^6\right)\right|_0^{\sqrt2/2} = 4\sqrt{5}\left(2\cdot\frac{1}{2} - 4\cdot\frac{1}{4} + 2\cdot\frac{1}{8}\right) = 4\sqrt{5}\left(1-1+\frac{1}{4}\right) = 4\sqrt{5}\cdot\frac{1}{4} = \sqrt{5}
        </md>,
        which matches the value found in
        <xref ref="example-ch04-02-ex-15-3"/>, exactly as guaranteed by the
        Independence of Parametrization.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
